Here is the complete example.
```javascript
class LoadError extends Error {
  constructor(message, cause) {
    super(message, {cause});
    this.name = "LoadError";
  }
}
function readLabel(raw, events) {
  try {
    const data = JSON.parse(raw);
    if (typeof data?.label !== "string") {
      throw new TypeError("label must be text");
    }
    return data.label;
  } catch (cause) {
    throw new LoadError("Cannot load label", cause);
  } finally {
    events.push("finished");
  }
}
const events = [];
try { readLabel('{"label":7}', events); }
catch (error) { console.log(error.name, error.cause instanceof TypeError); }
console.log(events.join(","));
```
Output:
```text
LoadError true
finished
```
The numeric label fails the text check. `catch` wraps that TypeError in a LoadError. The original error remains available as `error.cause`.
